  {
    "episode_id": 90728,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90728.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "You know what, I don't know this in the sire mode. It's kind of like back home, right? One day it's sunny and 60 some degrees and the next day we could get to foot of snow. So it's the uncertain, you know, seriously, I mean, it's the uncertainty of the path that we're on. And at the end of the day, because we have such a slim majority, the question then comes up is what is it that you could do in your conference, since you're in the majority to get the right policies passed. And the answer to that is, listen, if, gosh, if one or two people and completely unseat whatever it is that you decided to come forward with, you're always skating on the old thing back home, skating on thing nice in April, you don't know who's still gonna be there on the day it comes up for a vote, or who's even gonna allow it to come up for a vote. That's one of the things I think that, and again, not a criticism, but you know this, and I know this, sometimes we can't even get our conference to go ahead and agree to bring some legislation forward to vote on. So you can block even bringing the legislation up just by saying we will not approve that. And at that point, the piece of legislation, whatever it is, whatever has been worked out, all it dies because it never gets a chance to hit sunlight. I think we face that right now. It's not a criticism of my part, by the way, of the two parties. It's very easy to see on the Democrat side, they are going to follow one after the other, whatever their leadership puts forward. Democrats are Republicans, on the other hand, of holy smokes. It was like when I was raising my wife and I raised our four kids, and you wanted to go out someplace and grab a bite to eat.",
