    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "not get all six people to agree on where they wanted to go to eat. So that what usually happens is, well, you know what, it doesn't matter anymore. It's no longer in our time. We passed where we walked away from that. And I don't mean to reduce it down to that, but I think one of the hardest things for me when I came in here to serve and to represent Pennsylvania at 16th congressional district is, well, why is it? And I think there's a lot of independent thinking that goes on within the Republican Party. And those members stay with that thinking regardless of the way leadership would like it to go. On the other hand, across the aisle, the Democrats, and I have friends that I sit with on these committees, they disagree with the policies that are being pushed, but they will never stand up and say no. I don't accept this and for the district I represent, those people that I work for, they wouldn't accept this either. Now, on any other hand, Republicans will fight and argue with each other to get what they think is the perfect piece of legislation and the Democrats will say whatever leadership draws is what we're going to do, which Some of this wrong-headed thinking that's out there, and I'm not going to debate that with any of our folks back home. But some of it we look at and we just shake our heads and say, how do people come up with this type of thinking? So that's the big difference. And we've got this issue now that We've got a death feeling that's approaching very quickly. And the question is, what do you do?",
