    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90728.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "I'm not trying to drag this out. I'm just trying to, it's much more difficult than people think. I have the greatest admiration for what Ukraine is trying to do. I also have the greatest questions about what is it once we send them money. I would rather send them hard assets. We left 80 billion dollars worth of assets in Afghanistan. We left. It would have been nice to see that still be used and be used for the right reasons. But we haven't done that. And so when we look at just money, the first obligation I have, the first obligation the President of the United States has, is to protect the homeland. When I see that we have turned a blind eye to our borders, by the way, not just the southwestern border, but that 5,500 miles of northern border, which is almost totally unprotected right now. And that is the easiest access into America. I look at all these these fires that are burning. And the question is, where do you put it? Where do you put your energy? Where do you put your dollars? And so far, because we've been through this and a lot of my friends have lost sons and daughters. In the battles we have been fighting the last couple of decades, we want to really be careful with what we do with our men and women in uniform. I want to make sure that we don't just put them in the position of danger without having really good reason and knowing that we have allies that are willing to put their shoulder to the wheel with us. I want to see how many of them are willing to go ahead and sacrifice their youth.",
